<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menander I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 165–130 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Milinda, Menander Soter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menander I was the best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> king, ruling much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and Gandhara from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a> in the mid-2nd century BCE. His coins are found widely in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and he led campaigns into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>. Buddhist tradition remembers him as King Milinda, whose dialogue with the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a> is recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milindapanha">Milindapanha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menander-i.jpg" alt="Menander I" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menander_I,_portrait_from_coinage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Milinda</b> Panha describes Menander reviewing his fourfold army of elephants, cavalry, bowmen and foot soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indo-Greek king <b>Menander</b>, who ruled in the second century BCE, is remembered in a Buddhist text, the Questions of King Milinda, as a ruler who debated with the monk Nagasena and ended as his disciple.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
